lesson: 123
title: Counting in a repeating pattern
level: primary-1
stars: 0
explain: A pattern repeats the same group again and again. Count how many groups there are, then count in each group.
explain: Beads go 2 red, 3 blue, 2 red, 3 blue, 2 red, 3 blue: that is 3 groups, so there are 3 + 3 + 3 = 9 blue beads.
explain: If you know all the beads instead, one group has 2 + 3 = 5 beads, and 15 beads make 3 groups.
vars: a=name r=1..3 b=1..3 m=2..5
let: n = m * (r + b)
need: m * b >= 2
answer: m * b
exercise: {a} threads beads in the order {r} red, {b} blue, {r} red, {b} blue, and so on. {a.He} threads {n} beads, and the last blue group is complete. How many blue beads are there?
exercise: {a} draws a row of {r} red and {b} green dots and repeats it {m} times. How many green dots does {a.he} draw?
exercise: Tiles on a path go {r} white, {b} grey, {r} white, {b} grey, and so on. There are {m} groups of white and grey tiles. How many grey tiles are there?
exercise: Flags in a row go {r} yellow, {b} green, again and again. There are {n} flags, and the row ends with a full group of green flags. How many green flags are there?
exercise: A train has cars painted {r} red, then {b} blue, and the colours repeat {m} times. How many blue cars does the train have?
test: A fence is painted in stripes: {r} white, {b} blue, {r} white, {b} blue, and so on. There are {n} stripes, and the fence ends with a whole blue part. How many stripes are blue?
test: {a} lays tiles: {r} white, {b} black, {r} white, {b} black, … {a.He} stops after the {m.ordwords} group of black tiles. How many black tiles has {a.he} laid?
test: How many blue beads are among {n} beads strung as {r} red, {b} blue, again and again, if the string ends right after a blue group?
exercise: {a} strings beads: {r} red, then {b} blue, again and again. {a.He} strings {n} beads and ends with a whole blue group. How many beads are blue?
exercise: {a} paints a pattern of {r} red and {b} yellow stars and repeats it {m} times. How many yellow stars are there?
exercise: On a pavement in London the slabs go {r} white, {b} grey, {r} white, {b} grey, and so on, in {m} complete groups. How many slabs are grey?
exercise: A row of {n} flags at a fair in Texas goes {r} red, {b} white, over and over, and finishes with a full set of white flags. How many white flags are in the row?
exercise: A toy train has carriages coloured {r} red, then {b} green, repeated {m} times. How many carriages are green?
exercise: Counters are laid as {r} yellow and {b} blue, and the pattern is laid down {m.words} times. How many counters are blue?
exercise: How many blue cups are there if {a} stacks {r} red and {b} blue, and does this {m} times in all?
exercise: Bunting in a Dublin street repeats the colours {r} green, {b} orange. There are {n} flags, ending with a full orange group. How many flags are orange?
test: {a} builds a tower of {n} blocks: {r} white, {b} black, then again and again, ending with a complete black part. How many black blocks are in the tower?
test: A necklace has {m} repeats of the pattern {r} pink, {b} purple. How many purple beads does it have?
test: Count the blue ones: {r} red, {b} blue, {r} red, {b} blue, … for {n} beads in total, the last group being blue.
test: Fence posts in Canterbury, New Zealand, are painted {r} white, {b} green, and so on. After the {m.ordwords} green group the fence stops. How many posts are green?
test: In a parade in Sydney the children march in a repeating line: {r} with drums, then {b} with flags. The line has {m} such groups. How many children carry flags?
test: {n} tiles are set in the order {r} white then {b} blue, again and again, and the row ends on a complete blue group. How many tiles are blue?
exercise: beads go {r} red {b} blue, {a} did it {m} times. how many blue
exercise: tiles {r} white {b} grey over n over, {m+1} lots, no wait, {m} lots. how many grey
exercise: my mate says {m*(r+b)} blue beads for {r} red {b} blue done {m} times. nah that's all of em, how many blue ones
exercise: {a} strung {n} beads, {r} red then {b} blue again n again, ends on a full blue bit. got em down the road for {m+3} bucks. how many blue
test: flags go {r} yellow {b} green, {n} flags all up, ends w/ a full green group. how many green ones
test: train's carriages {r} red {b} blue, repeat {m+2} times, no sorry, {m} times. how many blue carriages
test: my bro counted {m*(b+1)} blue for {r} red {b} blue done {m} times. that's wrong innit, how many blue?
exam: Beads are threaded in a recurring sequence of {r} red beads followed by {b} blue beads, the sequence being completed exactly {m} times. Determine the number of blue beads employed.
exam: It is given that a path is paved in a repeating arrangement of {r} white tiles followed by {b} grey tiles, and that {m} complete groups are laid. Ascertain the number of grey tiles.
exam: Whereas {a} threads {n} beads in the order {r} red, {b} blue, recurring, and the final group of blue beads is complete, state the number of blue beads.
exam: Compute the number of green dots drawn, given that a row of {r} red dots and {b} green dots is reproduced {m} times in succession.
exercise: For Grandad's "celebration of life", {a} lays flowers in a row: {r} white, then {b} yellow, and repeats it {m} times. How many yellow flowers does {a.he} lay?
exercise: Granny is "a bit forgetful these days", so {a} helps fill her pill box for {m} days, with these tablets for each day: {r} white, {b} blue. How many blue tablets go in?
test: bloody hell, the "pre-loved" necklace is just cheap beads: {r} red then {b} blue, over and over, {n} beads, ends on a full blue lot. how many blue
